points.explanation:
Many Japanese verbs come in pairs. A **transitive** verb has someone acting on an object marked with {を}: {私[わたし]がドアを開[あ]けた} 'I opened the door.' An **intransitive** verb describes the thing changing by itself, marked with {が}: {ドアが開[あ]いた} 'The door opened.'

Common pairs (transitive / intransitive):

- {開[あ]ける} / {開[あ]く}, {閉[し]める} / {閉[し]まる}
- {始[はじ]める} / {始[はじ]まる}, {止[と]める} / {止[と]まる}
- {つける} / {つく}, {消[け]す} / {消[き]える}
- {入[い]れる} / {入[はい]る}, {出[だ]す} / {出[で]る}
- {落[お]とす} / {落[お]ちる}, {壊[こわ]す} / {壊[こわ]れる}

Japanese often prefers the intransitive when describing what happened without focusing on who did it, which can sound less like blaming: {コップが割[わ]れた} 'The glass broke.'
